From 8e659ffc132e539dc3fe3b73543dafb734d8791d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hibreak <hibreak@localhost>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 13:09:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] e-ink: unlock and fingerprint signals for the panel hold, no
 SystemUI animations

With debug.sf.eink_motion (and debug.sf.eink_sleep, default on), system_server
tells SurfaceFlinger (frameworks/native EinkMotion) about two more steps that
rearrange the whole screen, as PhoneWindowManager already does for sleep and
wake-up (vendor.xrz.hibreak_sleep):
- KeyguardController writes vendor.xrz.hibreak_unlock (uptime ms) when the
  keyguard goes away on the default display, or is hidden without going away
  first (a fingerprint match that woke the phone: SystemUI hides it at once,
  seen 2026-11-24): the lock screen then goes to home or the app in one panel
  update.
- The keyguard's fingerprint authentication writes vendor.xrz.hibreak_finger:
  the time of the last acquire with a finger on the sensor, 0 once the sensor
  answered without a match (rejected, lifted, error, cancelled); a match
  leaves the time for the unlock that follows. The side sensor is the power
  button, so a press with an enrolled finger is a wake-up and then an unlock;
  SurfaceFlinger keeps the wake-up picture until the sensor has answered.
  The MICROARRAY HAL's vendor acquires (2026-11-24): 1 capture armed with no
  finger, 2 finger down, 3 lifted (debug.sf.eink_finger.idle_code,
  .lifted_code).

- The power manager's Notifier writes vendor.xrz.hibreak_sleep itself when
  the default display group starts waking up or going to sleep, before it
  posts the policy calls to the main thread. A fingerprint wake-up kept that
  thread busy for ~470 ms, and the lock screen reached the panel before
  PhoneWindowManager's write (which stays).

With debug.sysui.eink_palette and debug.sysui.eink_animations=0 (opt-in; the
default 1 keeps animations on, owner's decision), SystemUI runs with the
animator duration scale at 0 (this process only; put back to 0 when the global setting changes).
The lock screen's animations (clock doze animators 4 x 819 ms, status bar
fade-in, navigation bar insets, scrims, 217-732 ms animators) kept the shade
redrawing at ~50 fps for ~1 s after every wake-up: the wake hold never saw a
still screen, let the panel go at its 450 ms limit with the lock screen
half-drawn and sent the rest as FAST updates. A fingerprint unlock from the
sleep screen showed the lock screen clock for a moment on its way to home.
AndroidX's animators (androidx.core.animation: the light-reveal scrim, the
notifications' wake-up, the keyguard status bar, 500 ms each) have a scale of
their own, which the framework's doesn't reach: it is set to 0 by reflection,
and proguard.flags keeps the setter and the field from R8. With only the
framework's scale they still held every wake-up to the limit.
With animations on, a power-button wake runs to wake_max_ms with the lock
screen still animating, as before.

Change-Id: Id957a993d73781e3fdf3a6684d212ae6689648ea
